# Context:
This page discusses challenges faced by AEO teams. It highlights the importance of having a clear and consistent content strategy, and avoiding overly broad or generalized content.

# Text:
CHALLENGE: "There's so much noise"
Across the board, AEO teams struggle with the sheer
scale of the web, and the amount of content they
need to consider, monitor, and update. The larger an
organization is, the more content they tend to have,
and the more difficult it is to track.
“Our team is always thinking about the
challenge of making sure our content is
discoverable amongst all the noise, and the
competition is only going to grow,” says
Kirsty Hulse, SEO Consultant and CEO of
Roar! Training.
On top of that, many AEO teams are held back by
their reliance on generalized content. When content is
broad, rather than specific, the search engines will
struggle to understand the topics.
"If content is really broad, like 'What is
digital transformation?', then search
engines and AIs will not understand that
you are an expert and that it's relevant to
your business," says Lily Ray, Senior
Director, SEO & Head of Organic Research
at Amsive Digital.
Many AEO teams are using similar, if not
identical, content to other companies. This is
especially true when organizations rely on third-party
content providers.
"AEO teams who are using commoditized
content have no chance of showing up in
search," says Bill Slawski, Director of SEO
Research at Go Fish Digital.

